{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And you shall place upon the net.</b> Meaning, to carry it. And Rabbi Yechiel, of blessed memory, raised a difficulty from what we say in tractate Shabbat: one who carries out a burden [in the public domain] above ten handbreadths is liable, for such was the carrying of the sons of Kehat, who carried the altar, and it was entirely above ten handbreadths. And a proof is brought there accordingly, for they had a tradition that anything carried on poles is a third above and two-thirds below, and the height of the Levites is ten cubits including the head. If so, at the very least from the shoulder downward was nine cubits, and the height of the altar was nine cubits excluding the horns. And when it is two-thirds below, that is six cubits, there are still from there to the ground three cubits, which is eighteen handbreadths — thus you learn that it was carried entirely above ten handbreadths. And now it is difficult: how can he bring a proof from the altar, which is two-thirds below, when it is written ״והיתה הרשת עד חצי המזבח״ and ״ועשית על הרשת ארבע טבעות נחושת״ — with which it was carried, implying that the altar was only half below? And one may answer that the Talmud brings a proof from the tradition that it is only a third above, and all the more so here, where half was above. And moreover, even without this tradition, one could have proven that one who carries out a burden above ten [handbreadths] is liable, as it is written ״ועשית על הרשת״ etc. — or alternatively, one could have said that the rings were very broad, so that not even a third remained above, and on this account it brought the tradition, etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:04:12.542785703Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}